<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Henry was Prince of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> from 1741. With his architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-joachim-stengel">Friedrich Joachim Stengel</a> he rebuilt Saarbrücken as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence, and he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and ironworks in the Saar, laying foundations for the region's later industry. His work was continued by his son Louis, the last ruling prince.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-henry-prince-of-nassau-saarbrucken.jpg" alt="William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Bergholz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilhelmHeinr007-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
